---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'
---

<Layout title="Nanovision | Source Code">
  <main class="pt-24 pb-16 min-h-screen bg-background">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">Source Code</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-muted-foreground mb-8">
        Accessing your source code allows Nanovision to provide context to the coverage data.
      </p>

      <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
        <h3>Why is source code needed?</h3>
        <p>
          While coverage reports tell us <em>which</em> lines were hit, they don't tell us <em>what</em> those lines contain.
          By reading your source files, Nanovision can:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Display the actual code in HTML reports.</li>
          <li>Syntax highlight the code for better readability.</li>
          <li>Show context around covered/uncovered lines.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Configuration</h3>
        <p>
          You simply need to point Nanovision to the root directory of your source code using the <code
            >-sourcedirs</code
          > flag or configuration option.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
